  <h1>Holding the Balloon <span class="tag tag-beginner">Beginner · Position 2</span></h1>

  <p>Holding the Balloon is the most classic health posture in Master Lam's system. Both hands rise to heart height, forming a round embrace as if holding a large balloon. Building on Wu Chi, this posture engages the arms and begins to cultivate the energy pathways of the upper body.</p>

  <div class="callout">
    <h4>📖 Book Reference</h4>
    <p>《The Way of Energy》Part Two, Chapters 4-5: Preparing for Energy / Growing Like a Tree</p>
  </div>
  <div class="callout">
    <h4>📺 BBC Reference</h4>
    <p>《Stand Still Be Fit》Days 3-4: Holding the Balloon instruction and deepening</p>
  </div>

  ---

  <h2>Practice Guide: Step by Step</h2>

  <h3>Feet, Knees, and Body</h3>
  <ul class="key-points">
    <li>Same as Wu Chi: feet shoulder-width apart, knees slightly bent and not beyond toes, sitting-not-sitting</li>
    <li>Toes forward, gripping the ground like tree roots</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Arms and Hands</h3>
  <ul class="key-points">
    <li>Raise both hands to <strong>heart height</strong> (not too high, not too low)</li>
    <li>Form a round embrace, as if holding a large balloon</li>
    <li>Appropriate distance between the hands — neither squeezing the balloon (too close) nor letting it drop (too far)</li>
    <li>Arms maintain a natural rounded arc — do not straighten them</li>
    <li>Fingers slightly spread, as if a marble rests on each fingertip, maintaining a subtle sense of connection</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Mental Imagery</h3>
  <ul class="key-points">
    <li>Imagine two small balloons under the armpits supporting the arms</li>
    <li>Imagine a balloon chair under the pelvis supporting the body (sitting-not-sitting)</li>
    <li>Crown of the head floating lightly above the shoulders</li>
  </ul>

  ---

